  "title": "Shielding is a law of coins that reach two places",
  "subject": "Whether the dual-pair identity of lone contradiction lines survives at three places",
  "conclusion": "In twisted fair worlds on tori, a lone contradiction line has the same count in the two twists odd on its class whenever each coin decides a contact on two places, a theorem on every torus. Exact enumeration of the smallest tori finds every pair not related by symmetry unequal once one coin decides three places, through a three-place relation or two clauses sharing it: the line falls at a coin's reach, not clause width.",
